  "historical_output_text": " Imagine you're standing in your backyard on a warm summer day. You notice the sun is high in the sky and the sky is clear. The air feels warm and dry, and you can see trees and buildings cast sharp shadows. This weather pattern you're experiencing is called a \"heat dome.\"\n\nA heat dome occurs when a high-pressure system develops over an area. High pressure typically means clear skies and calm winds, which are good for baking in the sun. But in this case, the high pressure is also strong, causing air to sink and warm up as it descends from the atmosphere. As the air sinks, it compresses and warms, making the air even hotter than usual.\n\nThe clear skies and calm winds mean there's no cloud cover or wind to cool things off, so the heat from the sun can easily reach the ground and warm",
